# PLAN3_SEC — TimeCap now_ms and no user-file shell

File: openOODA/audit/plan3_sec.oot
Wave 3 ATTACK. Ship 3. Worktree `/home/jeryd/.grok/worktrees/ooda-bench`.

Logline: Bench times with now_ms under TimeCap, starts oodac through find_oodac and sq, and does not sh the user file.
Setup: Attack `cli/cmd_more.oo` and `cli/main.oo`. `host_run.oo` and `find_compiler.oo` are read-only. Dirty parent is read-only.
Beats:
  1. Search for a date or bash timer. Remove it and FAIL if it exists.
  2. Confirm TimeCap is used only for now_ms.
  3. Confirm check and run use find_oodac plus sq. Confirm the user file is not a sh script.
  4. Record PASS or FAIL.

## 0. Verdict

PASS

No `date`. No bash timer. No product edit. No removal.
TimeCap is a capability token. It grants wall-clock access.
`now_ms` is the only reader of that token in bench.
Check and run use `find_oodac`, `refuse_cli`, `find_src_root`, and `sq`.
The user path is one quoted operand to `oodac check` or `oodac run`.
The user path is not the `sh -c` script.

## 1. Attack: date and bash timer

A TimeCap token is the only legal clock for this ship.
A SysCap shell that runs `date`, `date +%s`, or a bash `SECONDS`
clock is a new timer. That is FAIL. This role must delete it.

Search on disk (ship write set only):

  `cli/cmd_more.oo`
  `cli/main.oo`
  `scripts/bench_smoke.oo`

Needles: `date +`, `date --`, `/usr/bin/date`, `$(date`,
`EPOCHREALTIME`, `EPOCHSECONDS`, `sleep_ms`.

Result: no matches in `cmd_more.oo` or `bench_smoke.oo`.
`main.oo` hits `outdated` only. That is a PM verb. It is not a clock.

`run_ooda_bench` takes `t0` and `t1` from `now_ms(time)`.
Optional `--run` takes `r0` and `r1` from `now_ms(time)`.
If a delta is negative, the print value is 0.
There is no `sys_exec` of `date`. There is no bash timer to remove.

## 2. Attack: TimeCap used only for now_ms

`cli/main.oo`:

  `pub fn main(fs: &FsCap, sys: &SysCap, time: &TimeCap, args: List[String])`
  `if cmd == "bench" { run_ooda_bench(fs, sys, time, args); return; }`

`time` goes only to `run_ooda_bench`. Other commands keep FsCap and SysCap.
That is least privilege (RULES 1.4).

`cli/cmd_more.oo`:

  `pub fn run_ooda_bench(fs: &FsCap, sys: &SysCap, time: &TimeCap, args: List[String])`

Uses of `time` in that function: four `now_ms(time)` calls.
Two wrap `oodac check`. Two wrap optional `oodac run`.
`time` is not an argument to `sys_exec`.
`sleep_ms` is absent.

`oodac_bin check cli/cmd_more.oo` prints `OK` and exits 0.
`oodac_bin check cli/main.oo` prints `OK` and exits 0.

## 3. Attack: sh of the user file

Plan: do not exec the bench target with `sh -c`.
Use the same check and run helpers as the product CLI.

`run_oodac_check` calls `process_exit`. Bench cannot wrap it
and still print ms. DE1 inlines the same shape as `host_run.run_oodac`.

On disk:

  `let oodac: String = find_oodac(fs);`
  `refuse_cli(sys, oodac);`
  `let src_root: String = find_src_root(oodac);`
  prefix = env + `sq(oodac)`
  `sys_exec(sys, "sh", "-c", prefix + " check " + sq(path));`
  optional: prefix + `" run "` + `sq(path)`

`sh -c` starts oodac. It does not start the user file.
There is no `sh PATH`. There is no `sh -c PATH`.
There is no `sh` of file bytes.

`sq` lives in `cli/host_run.oo`. A quote in the path becomes `'\''`.
A path with `;`, spaces, or `date +%s` stays one operand.
`host_run.oo` and `find_compiler.oo` are unmodified
(`git status --porcelain` is empty on both).

`refuse_cli` rejects a compiler whose strings contain
`pure .oo product CLI`. That blocks a CLI-as-oodac fork bomb.

## 4. Attack: new SysCap shell timer

SysCap is required to start oodac. That is the product check path.
A new SysCap timer would be `date` or a shell clock around the child.
The child string has no `date` and no `%s`.
Wall ms come from TimeCap only.

Bench does not set `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
It does not slurp or print file bytes.
It does not add Net, Process, or a new spawn API.

`--run` runs only after check returns Ok.
Check fail prints `ooda bench: check failed FILE` and exits 1.
No run line. No `ooda bench: ok`.

